  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe introduce a new method based on the *two-point local search* technique and *gradient information* to more efficiently explore the optimization space. \nThe core idea is to systematically improve solutions by transferring mass between two points while ensuring the integral constraint is maintained.\nThis contrasts with the previous stochastic local search, which might get trapped in local optima.\nWe reinterpret the original objective\u2020 and \u2207\u222b (overlap correlation) to create a bounded local optimization scheme.\nOur method leverages the fact that the minimum C corresponds to solutions with high symmetry and low correlation at many lags.\nWe start with the provided initial_h_values (n=300) and apply 2-point local search at each grid point, then use the best solution for cross-resolution comparison.\nOur key innovations are:\n1. A two-point search with gradient descent on overlap maps\n2. Focus on large negative correlation regions to maximize improvement\n3. Alternating refinement between grid resolutions\n4. Smarter lag selection based on correlation magnitude and structure\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe minimize the maximum overlap over 2*(n-1) lags, defined as:\n  C5 = max_k [ (1/n) * || h * circshift((1-h),k) ||_1 ]\nwith integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1\nGrid representation:\n  Parameters: n (grid size), h = (h\u2080, h\u2081, ..., h_{n-1})\n  Transformations:\n    Reprojection: h \u21a6 h_rescaled = clip(project_feasible(h, n))\n  Objective mapping:\n    Compute all 2n-1 shifts (k=-n+1 to n-1) but yield symmetric overlaps\n  Local modification:\n    For a pair (i,j), temporarily set h[i] = a, h[j] = b such that:\n      a + b = h[i] + h[j]\n    We search a, b \u2208 [0,1] that maximize immediate overlap reduction\n  Gradient estimation:\n    \u2207i \u2248 (overlap_map[k+j-n] - overlap_map[k-j+n]) * (dx/num_lags)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: parent_h (if available), grid size n (initially 300 if parent given)\n2. Projection repair function:\n      def project_feasible(h, n):\n          h = h.copy()\n          gap = n/2 - sum(h)\n          if abs(gap) < 1e-10: return h\n          if gap > 0:\n              h += gap/sum(1-h) * (1-h)\n          else:\n              h += gap/sum(h) * h\n          return np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n3. Objective computation:\n      dx = 2/n\n      c = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx * (2/n)  # Note: normalizing factor correction\n      return float(1/n * 1/n * np.max(c) * n)\n\nCore optimization:\n  while improvement_count < 1000 and time_left>5:\n    best_c = current best objective\n    buffer = random grid point index\n    Lag selection:\n      top_lag = argmax_lag in [-10,10] with largest |magnitude|\n    Gradient descent on h[buffer] only:\n      Debias top_lag contribution\n      Compute direction \u2207 overlap from sensitivity analysis\n      Bounds: new_value = clip(h[buffer] + delta, 0,1)\n    Progressively expose all lags if improved\n\nImplementation details:\n// Note: This is pseudocode, need concrete array operations for real arrays.\n1. Project grid to feasible space\n2. Compute objective including all required lags\n3. Apply revised two-point update with gradient guidance\n4. Compare new solution with current best\n5. If valid, reevaluate at new grid else repeat\n\nSearch choices:\n1. Initial parent: None if no parent found\n2. Default grid size: 300\n3. Local search parameters:\n      n_lags = 21 (\u00b110)\n      max_step = 0.2\n      restart_gap = 5000 iterations without improvement\n4. Stochastic elements:\n      \u03b1 = random in [0.01,0.99] for initial perturbation scale\n      \u03b2 = random lag in [-n,n] not excluded\n5. Termination:\n      1000 function evaluations OR deadline expires\n\nBudget and robustness:\n1. Time estimation for n=300: each eval \u2248 0.005s, 10k evals \u2248 50s\n2. Deadline actions:\n      - Check interval: 90% remaining deadline\n      - If improvement stalled > 500 steps: restart with large perturbation\n      - If no improvement: lower precision and try higher n-grid\n\nValidation and return:\n1. Check objective integrates to 1 (project_feasible ensures it)\n2. Return tuple (optimized_h, optimal_C5, n_used)\n\n// Bound notation explained:\n// The original objective has been re-expressed due to the finite-support discrete convolution answer\n\u2020 Original integrals are approximately represented by the discrete sum.\nThe full objective with length L has been normalized to better align with theoretical bounds.",
